    description: "FOUND 2026-08-28 while investigating WI-zumoz, whose stated mechanism turned out\nto be wrong. Write-up: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/zumoz_probe_08282026/RESULT.md\n\nTHE CATALOGUE SIDE IS FINE. Feeding well-formed synthetic edges to\nclassify_call, every one of these rows matches -- including with construct\n\"assignment\" and with no construct meta at all:\n    WebSocket.onmessage / onclose, EventSource.onmessage  -> all matched\nSo \"a method-kind row cannot match a property assignment\" is NOT the mechanism.\n\nTHE ANALYZER NEVER EMITS THE EDGE. Four fixtures through\n`hypergumbo io-boundaries`, fresh cache each, two positive controls firing in\nthe same runs:\n    fetch(...)                          function-keyed global   MATCHED (control)\n    localStorage.setItem / getItem      method on NAMED global  MATCHED (control)\n    x.open(...) / x.send(...)  x = new XMLHttpRequest()         no chain\n    ws.send(...)               ws = new WebSocket(...)  module scope   no chain\n    ws.addEventListener('message', cb)  a REAL method call      no chain\n    ws.onmessage = cb                   property assignment     no chain\n    TypeScript: const ws: WebSocket = new WebSocket(...); ws.send(...)  no chain\n\nTHE DISCRIMINATOR IS RECEIVER SHAPE, NOT ASSIGNMENT-VS-CALL: a NAMED GLOBAL\nreceiver (localStorage.x, where the receiver IS the module name) resolves; a\nCONSTRUCTED INSTANCE (ws = new WebSocket(...); ws.x) never does -- in JS or TS,\nat module or function scope, called or assigned.\n\njs_ts.py EXPECTS THE OPPOSITE. The JS_KNOWN_GLOBALS comment says constructor-\nstyle globals \"are typically `new`'d first and reach io-boundaries through the\ninstance path, not this fallback.\" _ParsedFile documents the inference it relies\non (\"Direct constructor calls: client = new Client() -> var_types['client'] =\n'Client'\"). The instance path does not arrive at the io-boundary layer.\n\nAFFECTED ROWS -- 11, across 4 modules and 4 boundaries, ALL INERT:\n    net_recv  WebSocket.addEventListener, WebSocket.onclose, WebSocket.onmessage\n    net_send  WebSocket.send\n    net_recv  EventSource.addEventListener, EventSource.onmessage\n    net_send  XMLHttpRequest.open, XMLHttpRequest.send,\n              XMLHttpRequest.setRequestHeader\n    ipc_recv  BroadcastChannel.addEventListener\n    ipc_send  BroadcastChannel.postMessage\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE ROW COUNT.\n  * The browser WebSocket/SSE RECEIVE surface is not \"thinner than the catalogue\n    suggests\" (WI-zumoz's reading) -- it is EMPTY. addEventListener, the row\n    that reading leans on, is equally unreachable.\n  * The whole XHR EGRESS surface is empty. XMLHttpRequest.open is also one of\n    WI-lunav's eight builder rows, so no finding could ever have existed at that\n    row to adjudicate -- a silent zero that would have read as \"no problem here\".\n  * BroadcastChannel.postMessage's net_recv -> ipc_send direction correction is\n    inert: the row is right and unreachable.\n\nNO FALSE-COVERAGE RISK TODAY, which is what keeps this P2 rather than P1: a call\nthat never classifies is never counted as EXAMINED (INV-buzab), and javascript's\ncatalogue status is `in_progress`, so it makes no module_completeness claim. The\ndamage is missing recall, not a false all-clear.\n\nFIXING IT ADDS RECALL, so it is band-stopped under WI-sivuz. Filed so the gap is\naddressable and countable rather than rediscovered a third time (INV-linub is\nthe same family one level over: capability is not recall)."  # 489e
    fields:  # 489e
      statement: "Every row in a shipped io_primitives catalogue is reachable by the analyzer that owns its language: if the source contains the call the row describes, an edge is emitted that the row can classify."  # 489e
      root_cause: "The JS/TS analyzer resolves a member access only when the receiver is a NAMED GLOBAL whose name is the module name (localStorage.setItem). A receiver bound by construction (ws = new WebSocket(...)) is never resolved to its type at the io-boundary layer, so no classifiable edge is emitted -- in JS or TS, at module or function scope, whether the member is called or assigned. js_ts.py's own JS_KNOWN_GLOBALS comment assumes the opposite, that these reach io-boundaries via the instance path."  # 489e
